From: twd@cs.brown.edu (Tom Doeppner)
Subject: Re: threads & asynch signals
Date: 1996/01/04
Message-ID: <4ch34n$c4g@cocoa.brown.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 134079603
references: <820765792snz@wsl.co.uk>
organization: Brown University Department of Computer Science
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc

In article <820765792snz@wsl.co.uk> dcb@wsl.co.uk writes:
>
>Hello there,
>
>Threads and asynch signals don't seem to mix. To quote the man page,
>
>In a multi-threaded environment, the sigaction function should only
>be used for the synch signals.
>
>Does anyone have any idea how one is supposed to write a real time
>application (ie uses SIGALRM and has a handler for it), and use
>the pthreads feature, on this *wonderful* OSF 3.2 ?
>
>There's a free beer in it.

You might use sigwait: mask off SIGALRM throughout the process, then
create a thread that calls sigwait, with SIGALRM set in the argument
mask.  The thread will block in the sigwait call and return only when
a SIGALRM occurs.  It's a much cleaner way of doing things than with
signal handlers.

Tom Doeppner
Brown University
twd@cs.brown.edu



