X-Deja-AN: 136505399
organization: NeoSoft Internet Services +1 713 968 5800
newsgroups: comp.mail.sendmail,comp.unix.osf.misc

I have inherited the administration duties for a system which
is a copy of another web and mail site.  There was no control
on users for picking their login ids, and there are many login
IDs which contain uppercase characters or are all uppercase.

I cannot send mail to anyone with such a login id.  Sendmail
reports (for example):

	sendmail -d25 Kunkee2
	Version 5.65v3.2
	sendto: Kunkee2
	   ctladdr=[NULL]
	Kunkee2... User unknown

Csh has no problem:
	> echo ~kunkee2
	Unknown user: kunkee2.
	> echo ~Kunkee2
	/usr/users/Kunkee2


If there are two login ids, such as 'Kunkee2' and 'kunkee2', mail
to 'Kunkee2' will arrive in 'kunkee2's mailbox.

In all my years administering Unix systems, it would never occur
to me to create a user ID with upper case characters in it.  It
is obvious that sendmail is downshifting the user ID before looking
it up, even though the final error message report the original
login ID given to it.


This problem has me pretty baffled.  Is it even legal?  Apparently
they had this working on another system, but this one was built
up from scratch and things won't work the way they did before.
If it is legal to use case-sensitive IDs with sendmail, how do
I get it to try the case-sensitive name first?

While a debatable practice, Unix does not seem to have a problem
handling this, so this is apparently specific to sendmail.  Eudora
can even come in an snarf mail correctly from a case-mixed login id.
But getting the mail to the spool directly in the first place is
the problem.  (My Linux system at home has the same behavior.)

Any insights will be greatly appreciated.  I'd prefer email replies,
but I will follow these groups, or post a summary of replies if requested.

Randy

