In article <4ed50t$o9f@rs18.hrz.th-darmstadt.de>,
Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt <leinberger@axp621.gsi.de> wrote:

> To my best knowledge the standard says that internet adresses are NOT
> case sensitive. So you even might be able to configure your local SW to
> be cases sensitive, but then you violate the standard. This will
> probably create a lot of trouble with the outside world.
> 
> So if you want to conform to the standard, it seems you indeed have to
> change to lower case user names.

This is incorrect. I checked RFCs 821 and 822 on this point only last week. 
The *domain* portion of a mail address is case insensitive but the specs
specifically say that case should be preserved in the username portion
of the address.

The only exception is that the special address "postmaster" must be
recognised in a case insensitive way (ie. mail to "postmaster",
"POSTMASTER" and "Postmaster" must all reach a human who deals with
problems with the mail service).

*However* I agree with the previous posters in this thread that it is a
good idea to avoid upper case or mixed case usernames on Unix systems if
it is at all possible. It will make life much easier in the long run.

Regards,
