In article <jrvalverde-3001962119590001@b12mc6.cnb.uam.es>,
jrvalverde@samba.cnb.uam.es (jr) wrote:

>    Pardon me for being alive, but,
> 
>    what's wrong with having mixed case names in any OS?

    If all the OSes in the world supported that, it'd be fine.  Since not
all of them do, then you have to worry about what will happen at one end
or the other (or anywhere in between) if you should happen to cross a link
that violates the RFC 822 rules regarding keeping case intact on the basis
that it might make a difference for the MTA or MUA on one end or the
other.

    And any time you have to worry about converting something that could
keep two people from talking (semi-permanently), you have to wonder
whether it really is worth the hassle to keep mixed case in usernames. 
Most of the time, the answer is no.  I certainly wouldn't work anywhere
that the answer was yes, just because I have an idea of how impossible it
could be to solve this problem for the entire Internet in general -- I've
already got ulcers, I don't need ULCERS.

